TPK Healy has made one file that can run on DOS 16, Win 32 (will also run o=
n Win 64), Linux arm or x86 32 or 64. The program is really a script (using=
 the above technique and some bash scripting) to unpack (from itself) a cop=
y of the C program compiled under the matching OS. The file script.txt is t=
he base, and the compiled programs are added to it via the linux commands: =
<pre>cat prog_dos16bit.exe | xxd -p \
| awk '{ print \" ::dos16bit \" $s \"\\r\" }' &gt;&gt; script.txt
cat script.txt prog_mswin_x86_32.exe prog_x86_32 prog_x86_64 \
prog_arm32 prog_arm64 &gt; ftsha256.bat</pre>
Once that is done, the offsets in the linux section must be updated to poin=
t to the start and encode the length of each program.=20

